From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: frames in the display's own colour space (WindowServer does not convert them)

What: the IOSurface present draws the guest's 8-bit SDR frames through a
table (ui/omacvm-dispcolor.h) into the window's display's colour space and
tags the surfaces with that space instead of sRGB. The main thread follows
the window's display (a move to another display, a new display profile,
displays changing) and counts each new space; the QEMU thread makes the
table for it in present_ctx (once per space, a few ms), checks it against
macOS's own conversion and makes the surfaces again (at once, at most once
a second, like a new depth). HDR (PQ), 10-bit, Extra brightness and the
boot logo stay as they were; so does a display that is sRGB itself, or
one whose profile the table cannot follow closely enough (the check: more
than 2 codes off), and everything after a failure (sRGB tag, as before).
OMACVM_GL_DISPLAY_COLOR=0 keeps the sRGB tag. qemu.log says once per
display what it does: "cocoa: display colour: frames converted to <name>
here (table N codes from macOS's own conversion), tagged with it", or why
not.

Why: tagged sRGB, WindowServer colour-matches the whole window on every
composite. Measured with a window like the VM's without a VM (MacBook Air
M2, full screen 2940x1840, work/window-path wsbench): WindowServer's GPU
time for it 79.6 ms/s at 30 frames a second tagged sRGB, 56.5 tagged with
the display's own space (= untagged); 154 vs 109 at 60. The VM showed the
same (vm-power: 77 ms/s, 53 untagged, for a 1080p video). Native apps
(Chrome) render in the display's space and tag their surfaces with it.
The surfaces actually there decide how a frame is drawn (the hold-off),
so no frame shows in the wrong colours.

Upstream: OmacVM only (QEMU's IOSurface present is OmacVM's).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/display/test-display-color.sh (rules, table, the
shader on Apple's software renderer against macOS's own conversion; with
the patched ui/cocoa.m: the wiring).
Applies after omacvm-cocoa-display-color-logic.patch and
omacvm-cocoa-extra-brightness.patch (before omacvm-cocoa-no-app-nap.patch).
